        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
There is a need to identify thresholds of muscle performance, below which mission task completion is likely to be impaired. This is important for many reasons such as informing the requirements for future exploration exercise hardware and prescriptions so that equipment can be scaled to fitness needs, predicting crewmember's readiness to perform the more demanding exploration tasks that may occur at any point during the mission as well as to inform preflight exercise training needs. This study used a standard set of critical mission tasks that could be used to threshold cardiovascular, skeletal muscle, and sensorimotor performance. The study was more operational than hypothesis driven. Previously validated techniques were used to identify fitness thresholds for performance of a new set of mission-related tasks. The main objective was to identify thresholds of muscle performance, below which task performance is impaired. A variety of muscle performance measurements were evaluated.
<P>
APPROACH: <BR>
While the ground test subjects were performing mission tasks that are physically challenging and mission-critical, various measures were taken.  First, measure predictor variables was measured, such as maximal strength, power, and power endurance in volunteers similar in age and physiologic capacity to the astronaut corps. Subsequently, the subjects performed the selected critical mission tasks. To evaluate the effects of muscle strength/power on task performance, the weighted suit model was used to decrease subjects’ relative strength (strength/body weight ratio); subjects completed each critical mission task with an additional 0, 20, 40, 60, and 80% of their bodyweight. Time to completion for each trial was measured.  Both fractional polynomial regression and spline regression techniques were used to address the hypothesis that a strength (power)/bodyweight threshold exists, below which performance decrements follow a markedly different and negative slope. Results from these models will enable the identification of the muscle strength/power thresholds below which performance of mission tasks is impaired.
<P>
RESULTS:<br>
Fitness thresholds for four space explorations tasks were identified using 23 physiological input variables. Baseline muscle performance measures and exploration task testing were completed on 65 subjects. Subjects performed each of the four exploration tasks under five different loaded conditions. The relationship between strength/weight versus task performance time was characterized. Strength thresholds below which task completion time (i.e., task performance) became markedly impaired were identified. 
<p>
        </div>

